## 2026-08-15: Reproducible WebDriverAgent IPA artifacts

- Changed the GitHub Actions WDA build from `main`-only to every pushed branch,
  with per-ref concurrency so a newer revision cancels an obsolete build.
- Pinned the runner to `macos-15` with Xcode 16.4 and pinned Appium
  WebDriverAgent 16.2.0 to commit
  `cd3efe0c27b0924cc34488843e8cd7b6c6f775fa` instead of building an
  unversioned moving upstream branch.
- The workflow now checks out and verifies the triggering SIKSIK SHA before
  building WDA. Each unsigned IPA embeds a versioned provenance record with
  the SIKSIK revision, WDA revision/version, repository, and Actions run
  identity. The uploaded artifact also includes the same sidecar record and a
  SHA-256 checksum, and the workflow verifies both copies before upload.
- Clarified that WDA is the iOS UI bridge; acquisition, selectors, crawl limits,
  and ingestion changes remain host-side SIKSIK code and are not compiled into
  the IPA. Signing/install remains the existing local AltServer step.
- The setup now identifies the tracked IPA as a legacy fallback and prefers a
  verified download in `~/wda`. When both the regular IPA and a stripped-dSYM
  cache exist, install/runtime selection uses the newer file so a stale cache
  cannot silently override a newly downloaded Actions artifact.
- Verification: `actionlint` 1.7.7, YAML and source-pin JSON parsing, Bash
  syntax and newest-IPA selection smoke, workflow diff checks, IPA
  ZIP/checksum validation, and embedded-versus-sidecar provenance comparison
  passed. The macOS/Xcode compile remains the workflow's required release gate
  after these local changes are committed and pushed.

## 2026-08-14: Reliable deleted/cache nudity and bounded iOS Photos recovery

- Fixed a false-clean cache path in nudity analysis. Detector, inference, and
  video-codec failures now return a non-cacheable outcome, while a completed
  analysis with no trigger remains cacheable. The analysis fingerprint is now
  `v14` and includes local `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` availability, so an earlier run
  without a usable sampler cannot suppress a later valid video result.
- QUICK and FULL Android recovery now both inspect deleted-media cache sources
  using their existing mode-specific limits. A versioned
  `cache_scan_completed` marker prevents an older trash-only manifest from
  being reused as though cache discovery had run. Cache source/capture counts
  are additive progress and HTML-report fields.
- A code-only smoke against the connected Infinix completed QUICK cache
  discovery with 1 gallery-cache source and 25 bounded preview artifacts; the
  expected budget warning was reported and the temporary smoke output was
  removed. Re-analysis of the previously recovered video produced a pending L4
  nudity finding at confidence 0.514. No media was displayed manually.
- Reimplemented the useful additions from the separate `ios-media-puller`
  donor inside SIKSIK: Photos Hidden, Recently Deleted, V2 thumbnails, video
  keyframes, bounded `.ithmb` JPEG carving, and metadata-only ACHANGE/SQLite-WAL
  purge evidence. The donor remains reference-only; production has no import,
  runtime path, subprocess, symlink, or dependency outside the SIKSIK tree.
- Current DCIM and recovered Photos sources use the same QUICK 3-month and FULL
  6-month cutoff. Recovery has explicit UDID binding, count/byte/file/traversal
  limits, timeouts, hashed names, a strict manifest, hash verification, source
  provenance, and fail-soft composition with the existing AFC media flow.
  Prior-session output is never searched or copied into a new session.
- Hidden, recently deleted, recovered-cache, and purge-metadata artifacts enter
  the existing index, analysis (including image/video nudity), review,
  recommendation, timeline, dashboard labels, and report flow. Existing DCIM,
  documents, backup communications, and social acquisition remain additive and
  unchanged.
- API changes are backward-compatible optional progress fields; no database
  migration was required. Verification: backend 259 passed/4 conditional
  skips, Android app/automation unit-test and Kotlin compile tasks succeeded,
  frontend production build passed, Python compilation passed, and diff checks
  passed. Physical iOS acquisition was not run because no iPhone was detected.

