## 2026-08-14: Native Android trash/deleted-preview recovery in QUICK + FULL

- Reimplemented the useful non-root recovery behavior as native SIKSIK modules.
  The external donor project was used only to understand behavior and validate
  the device capability; there is no runtime import, path, subprocess, symlink,
  dependency, or staging connection to that project.
- Added bounded MediaStore trash and shared-storage trash discovery to QUICK and
  FULL. QUICK scans selected high-value shared folders with 25-item/512 MiB
  defaults; FULL scans all readable shared roots with 500-item/8 GiB defaults.
- FULL additionally validates gallery cache indexes/data, classic thumbnails,
  and thumbdata residue. Cache previews are emitted only when their MediaStore
  identity/source is absent and their JPEG/PNG/WebP structure validates.
- Recovery is additive after the existing Android provider completes. Existing
  agent inventory, preprocessing, selection/review ledger, direct transfer, and
  teardown remain unchanged. Verified recovered artifacts then use the normal
  indexing, hash, analysis (including nudity), review, recommendation, timeline,
  and report paths under source `recovered_trash`.
- Added a strict versioned manifest with hash/size verification, hashed artifact
  names, source/classification/confidence provenance, atomic writes, idempotent
  resume, bounded output/time/bytes/items, cancellation cleanup, and safe
  best-effort fallback when only recovery is unavailable. Raw device paths,
  content URIs, filenames, serials, and media content are not persisted in the
  recovery manifest or structured logs.
- Added a privacy-safe SIKSIK smoke runner that reports only state, counts,
  scopes, and hashes, and always removes temporary artifacts. On the connected
  authorized device, both QUICK and FULL completed with 2 verified trash
  payloads (101,233,120 bytes), zero warnings, and successful cleanup; no media
  was displayed. The device contained no validated orphan cache preview.
- Session progress, finding source labels, session picker, operator telemetry,
  dashboard, and HTML report use the existing SIKSIK contracts/components; no
  separate prototype result format or wireframe was introduced.
- No database migration was required. Backend suite: 244 passed, 4 conditional
  skips. Android app/automation Kotlin compilation and frontend production build
  both passed.

